var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"DIFFERENCES/#Chopper.jl-and-chopper-0.14.1","page":"Chopper.jl and chopper 0.14.1","text":"What is the same, what differs on purpose, and what was found in chopper while porting it.","title":"Chopper.jl and chopper 0.14.1"},{"category":"section","location":"DIFFERENCES/#Same-behaviour","page":"Chopper.jl and chopper 0.14.1","text":"With the same options and one thread in chopper, the output (records, bytes), the stderr messages and the exit status are identical; test/reference/ checks this on 35 cases and validation/ on real data. In particular:\n\nMean quality: error probabilities from a table of 10^(-q/10), summed left to right, -10 log10(mean), with Julia's exp10 and log10 (see below for the last bit).\nFilter order and activation: length, quality (only if -q > 0 or --maxqual < 1000), GC (only if --mingc or --maxgc is set), contaminants. GC counts G/C in either case over all bases, N included in the denominator.\nTrimming: the four strategies with chopper's tie rules (the best read segment prefers, among equal scores, the longest and then the first; a running sum of exactly zero is not reset); filters judge the untrimmed read and only -l is checked again on each segment, not with --trim-approach unset.\nHeaders: the trailing whitespace of the header line is removed, as chopper's FASTQ parser does; split reads get _segment_<n> on the read name, before any description or tab-separated tag.\nBase modification tags (--update-mods): MM/ML/MN rewritten with the same rules (htslib semantics, N counts every base, lower-case tag names, two-code lists, MM without ML, + before a skip count accepted like Rust's integer parser) and the same error messages; a broken tag is fatal and the output incomplete.\nCommand line: same options, defaults, warnings for ignored trimming options, errors for missing ones.","title":"Same behaviour"},{"category":"section","location":"DIFFERENCES/#Differences-on-purpose","page":"Chopper.jl and chopper 0.14.1","text":" chopper Chopper.jl\nOutput order with -t > 1 order in which threads finish (varies between runs) input order, for any number of threads\nUse from code command line only chopper(), read_segments, read_status, trim_ranges, ContaminantIndex, ...\nStatistics \"Kept N reads out of M reads\" ChopperStats: reads/bases read and written, count per failed filter, reads emptied by trimming; --summary\nTrimming options flags validated at run time (--cutoff with fixed-crop is a warning) one type per strategy; invalid combinations cannot be built in the API (the CLI keeps chopper's warnings)\nSegments 0-based (start, end) 1-based UnitRange\nErrors eprintln! + process::exit from any thread exceptions (ModTagError with the read name); the CLI turns them into chopper's messages and exit codes\nGlobal state counter of reads with MM tags none (stats.reads_with_mods)\n--inverse evaluates every filter, including the alignment against contaminants stops at the first failed filter: the read is kept anyway, so the result is the same\nEmpty reads (@r + empty sequence) the FASTQ parser fails; chopper panics (exit 101) and stops writing read normally; discarded by -l (≥ 1 by default)\nInput compression gzip, bzip2, xz for files (magic bytes or extension); stdin uncompressed gzip (zlib-ng), bzip2, xz, zstd, from magic bytes, files and stdin\nOutput stdout stdout, or -o file (gzip if .gz)\nContaminant screen minimap2 2.30 (C library, Rust crate) Minimap2.jl, a Julia port of minimap2 2.30 (CONTAMINANTS.md)\nFraction of matches for contaminants fixed 0.9 contaminant_fraction keyword (0.9)\n-t default 4 the Julia threads (julia -t N)\n\nNot supported (as in chopper): sequencing summary files. FASTQ records must fit on four lines (FASTX.jl); chopper's parser also accepts sequences split over several lines, which no current basecaller writes.","title":"Differences on purpose"},{"category":"section","location":"DIFFERENCES/#Found-in-chopper","page":"Chopper.jl and chopper 0.14.1","text":"Empty reads crash the run. A record with an empty sequence and quality makes the bio parser return IncompleteRecord; chopper panics (\"problem parsing fastq record\"), exits with status 101 and the records after it are lost (checked with the 0.14.1 binary).\nNon-deterministic output with more than one thread (documented upstream): on 200,000 reads -t 1 and -t 4 give different files with the same sorted lines.\nThe comment on --inverse in get_valid_segments says that all filters must be evaluated in inverse mode; since a read is kept as soon as one filter fails, the early exit is also valid there.\nFloating point and the platform: chopper's mean quality depends on the C library (powf for the table, log10), so chopper itself may classify a read exactly on a threshold differently on Linux and macOS. Against glibc, Julia's exp10 differs in 4 of the 256 table entries and log10 in about 2.5% of the arguments, always in the last bit; a port of glibc's functions was tried and dropped as unnecessary (no read of the validation data is affected).\nNot reproduced: chopper's comment on par_bridge/for_each_init (more initialisations than channels would drop reads with \"failed to send the read for writing\"); 1–32 threads kept the same 199,961 of 200,000 reads.","title":"Found in chopper"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Filtering-files","page":"API","text":"","title":"Filtering files"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Filters-and-single-reads","page":"API","text":"","title":"Filters and single reads"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Trimming-strategies","page":"API","text":"","title":"Trimming strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Quality-and-GC-content","page":"API","text":"","title":"Quality and GC content"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Contaminant-screen","page":"API","text":"","title":"Contaminant screen"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Modification-tags","page":"API","text":"","title":"Modification tags"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Input","page":"API","text":"","title":"Input"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Chopper.Chopper","page":"API","text":"Chopper\n\nFiltering and trimming of long-read FASTQ files (Oxford Nanopore, PacBio): a native Julia port of the Rust tool chopper 0.14.1 by Wouter De Coster.\n\nReads are filtered on mean quality, length, GC content and similarity to contaminant sequences, then optionally trimmed with one of four strategies (FixedCrop, TrimByQuality, BestReadSegment, SplitByLowQuality). The main entry point is chopper; the same filters are available per read through read_segments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.Chopper"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.ChopperStats","page":"API","text":"ChopperStats\n\nCounts returned by chopper:\n\nreads, bases: reads and bases read;\nsegments, kept_bases: records and bases written; segments is the number that chopper reports as \"Kept N reads\";\nkept_reads: reads with at least one written record;\npassed: reads that passed all filters (with inverse, these are the discarded ones);\nfailed_length, failed_quality, failed_gc, contaminants: reads by the first filter they failed;\ntrimmed_away: reads selected by the filters but with no segment left after trimming (or none long enough);\nreads_with_mods: reads that carried an MM tag (only counted with update_mods).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.ChopperStats"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.chopper","page":"API","text":"chopper(input, output = stdout; kwargs...) -> ChopperStats\n\nFilter and trim the reads of the FASTQ input and write the selected reads to output, as the command-line tool chopper does. input is a path or an IO (compressed input is recognised automatically, see open_fastq); output is a path (gzip-compressed if it ends in .gz) or an IO (not closed). Returns the counts as a ChopperStats.\n\nA path output is written to a temporary file in the same directory and renamed over output only when the run succeeds: after an error the file is missing or unchanged, never partial, also when the rename itself fails (for example on a file locked by another program). A replaced file keeps its permissions. Bytes already written to an IO (such as stdout) cannot be taken back: after an error that output is incomplete.\n\nAn input stream is not closed. Compressed input may be read ahead by a separate task: after an error chopper returns without waiting for a read of that stream still in progress (which could wait for data forever, as on a pipe); the task stops when the read returns or the stream is closed.\n\nKeywords:\n\nthe filters of ReadFilter: min_quality, max_quality, min_length, max_length, min_gc, max_gc, contaminants, contaminant_fraction; or filter, a ReadFilter, instead of them;\ntrim = NoTrim(): the trimming strategy, FixedCrop, TrimByQuality, BestReadSegment or SplitByLowQuality;\ninverse = false: write the reads that fail the filters instead;\nupdate_mods = false: rewrite the base modification tags MM, ML and MN for the trimmed reads (see rewrite_mod_tags); a read whose tags cannot be rewritten raises a ModTagError;\nthreads = Threads.nthreads(): tasks processing batches in parallel. The output is identical for any number of threads;\nbatch_bases = 4_000_000, batch_reads = 10_000: size of a batch.\n\nFilters judge the whole read; trimming is applied afterwards to the reads they select, and only the minimum length is checked again on each segment. In particular the mean quality is not recomputed for trimmed segments.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.chopper"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.ReadFilter","page":"API","text":"ReadFilter(; min_quality = 0, max_quality = 1000, min_length = 1,\n           max_length = typemax(Int), min_gc = nothing, max_gc = nothing,\n           contaminants = nothing, contaminant_fraction = 0.9)\n\nFilters applied to whole, untrimmed reads, as chopper's filtering options:\n\nkeyword chopper \nmin_quality, max_quality -q, --maxqual mean quality (mean_quality)\nmin_length, max_length -l, --maxlength read length; min_length is checked again on trimmed segments\nmin_gc, max_gc --mingc, --maxgc GC fraction, 0–1 (gc_content)\ncontaminants -c a ContaminantIndex or the path of a FASTA file\ncontaminant_fraction (fixed 0.9) see is_contaminant\n\nAs in chopper, the quality filter is evaluated only if min_quality > 0 or max_quality < 1000, and the GC filter only if min_gc or max_gc is set: with the defaults, reads of any quality (or GC content) pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.ReadFilter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.ReadStatus","page":"API","text":"ReadStatus\n\nOutcome of the filters for one read: PASSED, or the first filter it failed (FAILED_LENGTH, FAILED_QUALITY, FAILED_GC, CONTAMINANT). Filters run in that order, cheapest first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.ReadStatus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.read_segments","page":"API","text":"read_segments(seq, qual; filter = ReadFilter(), trim = NoTrim(), inverse = false)\n    -> Vector{UnitRange{Int}}\n\nRanges of the read that chopper would write: empty if the read is discarded, one range per written record otherwise (several when trim splits it).\n\nThe filters judge the whole read; then the trimming strategy is applied and each trimmed segment shorter than filter.min_length is dropped (not with NoTrim). With inverse = true the reads that fail a filter are kept and trimmed instead, as chopper's --inverse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.read_segments"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.read_status","page":"API","text":"read_status(filter::ReadFilter, seq, qual) -> ReadStatus\n\nFirst filter of filter that the read fails, or PASSED. seq and qual are the sequence and the raw Phred+33 quality line (bytes or strings).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.read_status"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.BestReadSegment","page":"API","text":"BestReadSegment(cutoff)\n\nKeep the single segment of the read with the highest score, where every base scores p(cutoff) - p(q) and p is the error probability of a Phred score (--trim-approach best-read-segment --cutoff cutoff). This is the modified Mott algorithm of Phred quality trimming: bases better than cutoff contribute positively, worse bases negatively. Among segments with the same score the longest (then the first) is kept. A read whose bases are all worse than cutoff is discarded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.BestReadSegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.FixedCrop","page":"API","text":"FixedCrop(head, tail)\n\nRemove head bases from the start and tail bases from the end of every read (--trim-approach fixed-crop --headcrop head --tailcrop tail). Reads not longer than head + tail are discarded. At least one of the two must be positive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.FixedCrop"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.NoTrim","page":"API","text":"NoTrim()\n\nKeep the whole read. With this strategy (the default) the minimum length is not checked a second time after trimming, as in chopper without --trim-approach.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.NoTrim"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.SplitByLowQuality","page":"API","text":"SplitByLowQuality(cutoff; window = 1)\n\nSplit reads at runs of at least window consecutive bases with a Phred score below cutoff, and keep the high-quality parts between them (--trim-approach split-by-low-quality --cutoff cutoff --split-window window). Shorter low-quality runs stay inside their segment; low-quality bases at the ends of a segment are removed. Each part must still reach the minimum read length to be written; parts are named <read>_segment_<n>. A window of 0 is treated as 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.SplitByLowQuality"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.TrimByQuality","page":"API","text":"TrimByQuality(cutoff)\n\nTrim bases with a Phred score below cutoff from both ends of the read, stopping at the first base with score >= cutoff on each side (--trim-approach trim-by-quality --cutoff cutoff). Low-quality bases inside the read are kept. A read without any base >= cutoff is discarded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.TrimByQuality"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.TrimStrategy","page":"API","text":"TrimStrategy\n\nAbstract type of the trimming strategies. A strategy s implements trim_ranges!(out, s, qual), which empties out, fills it with the ranges (UnitRange{Int}, 1-based) of the read to keep, in increasing order, and returns it. qual holds the raw Phred+33 bytes of the quality line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.TrimStrategy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.trim_ranges","page":"API","text":"trim_ranges(s::TrimStrategy, qual::AbstractVector{UInt8}) -> Vector{UnitRange{Int}}\n\nRanges of the read to keep under strategy s, from the raw Phred+33 bytes of its quality line (codeunits of the line, or Vector{UInt8}). An empty vector means that nothing is kept. See trim_ranges! for a version that reuses the output vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.trim_ranges"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.trim_ranges!","page":"API","text":"trim_ranges!(out::Vector{UnitRange{Int}}, s::TrimStrategy, qual) -> out\n\nIn-place version of trim_ranges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.trim_ranges!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.gc_content","page":"API","text":"gc_content(seq::AbstractVector{UInt8}) -> Float64\n\nFraction of G/C bases (either case) among all bases of seq, including N and other symbols in the denominator, as in chopper. An empty sequence gives NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.gc_content"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.mean_quality","page":"API","text":"mean_quality(qual::AbstractVector{UInt8}) -> Float64\n\nMean quality of a read, from the raw Phred+33 bytes of its quality line, computed as chopper (and NanoFilt) do: the Phred scores are converted to error probabilities, averaged, and the mean probability is converted back to a Phred score, -10 log10(mean(p)). This is lower than the arithmetic mean of the scores whenever the scores vary.\n\nThe sum runs left to right, as in chopper. The result can differ from chopper's in the last bit (Julia's exp10 and log10 against the C library's), which matters only for a read whose mean quality lies exactly on a threshold. An empty read gives NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.mean_quality"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.phred_to_probability","page":"API","text":"phred_to_probability(q::Integer) -> Float64\n\nError probability 10^(-q/10) of the Phred score q (0–255).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.phred_to_probability"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.ContaminantIndex","page":"API","text":"ContaminantIndex(fasta; k = 19, w = 19)\nContaminantIndex(names, sequences; k = 19, w = 19)\n\nMinimap2 index of contaminant sequences (e.g. the lambda phage control DNA of ONT kits, or a host genome) for is_contaminant and the contaminants option of chopper and ReadFilter. Reads are mapped with the minimap2 preset lr:hq and base-level alignment, as chopper does; k and w override the preset's minimizer length and window.\n\nThe index is only read while mapping and can be shared by any number of tasks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.ContaminantIndex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.best_match_length","page":"API","text":"best_match_length(index::ContaminantIndex, seq) -> Int\n\nLargest number of matching bases (\"match length\", PAF column 10) over the alignments of the read seq (bytes or string, either strand) to the contaminant sequences of index; 0 if the read does not map.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.best_match_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.is_contaminant","page":"API","text":"is_contaminant(index::ContaminantIndex, seq; min_fraction = 0.9) -> Bool\n\nWhether the read seq aligns to a contaminant with at least min_fraction × length(seq) matching bases, the criterion of chopper's --contam filter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.is_contaminant"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Chopper.ModTagError","page":"API","text":"ModTagError\n\nBase modification tags of a read that cannot be rewritten: malformed MM, ML or MN tags, MM and ML with different numbers of calls, an MM tag that refers to more bases than the read has, an MN tag with a different read length, or ML without MM. Fields: kind, one of :malformed_mm, :malformed_ml, :malformed_mn, :count_mismatch, :beyond_sequence, :stale_mn, :ml_without_mm; msg; read, the read name (empty if not known).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.ModTagError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.rewrite_mod_tags","page":"API","text":"rewrite_mod_tags(fields, seq, range) -> Vector{String}\n\nRewrite the base modification tags among the tab-separated SAM tag fields of a read with sequence seq (bytes or string) so that they describe the subsequence seq[range]:\n\nMM:Z: keeps only the calls inside range, with recomputed skip counts;\nML:B:C keeps the probabilities of those calls;\nMN:i: becomes length(range); it must equal the length of seq.\n\nOther fields are returned unchanged, including tags that trimming also invalidates (qs, ns, ts, du, ...). Tag names are matched without regard to case (Mm/Ml of older basecallers). Throws ModTagError if the tags are malformed or do not describe seq.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.rewrite_mod_tags"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Chopper.open_fastq","page":"API","text":"open_fastq(path::AbstractString) -> TranscodingStream\nopen_fastq(io::IO) -> TranscodingStream\n\nOpen a FASTQ file or stream for reading, decompressing it if needed. The format is recognised from the first bytes, not from the file name: gzip (always available, also multi-member files such as those of pigz or bgzip), bzip2, xz and zstd (with the packages CodecBzip2, CodecXz and CodecZstd installed). Closing the returned stream closes path's file, or io.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Chopper.open_fastq"},{"category":"section","location":"#Chopper.jl","page":"Home","text":"Filtering and trimming of long-read FASTQ files (Oxford Nanopore, PacBio) in native Julia: a port of chopper 0.14.1 by Wouter De Coster (Rust; the successor of NanoFilt and NanoLyse). No external programs: the contamination screen, which chopper delegates to minimap2, uses Minimap2.jl, a native port of the same minimap2 2.30.\n\nThis package is part of an experimental effort to use AI to port bioinformatics and quantitative ecology packages to Julia, with the goal of creating a unified environment for high performance scientific computing.\n\nReads are filtered on mean quality, length, GC content and similarity to contaminant sequences, then optionally trimmed with one of four strategies. With the same options the output is byte-identical to chopper's (see Validation), and it does not depend on the number of threads.\n\nJulia ≥ 1.10 (tested with 1.12). Dependencies: FASTX.jl (FASTQ/FASTA parsing), CodecZlibNG.jl (gzip, via zlib-ng), TranscodingStreams.jl, StringViews.jl, PrecompileTools.jl. Optional: CodecBzip2, CodecXz, CodecZstd for bzip2, xz and zstd input.","title":"Chopper.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"This package is not registered in the Julia General registry, and neither is its dependency Minimap2.jl. Install both from GitHub, Minimap2.jl first:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/alexology/Minimap2.jl\")\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/alexology/Chopper.jl\")\n\nor, from local copies, Pkg.develop(path = \"path/to/Minimap2.jl\") and then Pkg.develop(path = \"path/to/Chopper.jl\"). With Julia ≥ 1.11 Project.toml also points to ../Minimap2.jl ([sources]), so a checkout next to Minimap2.jl works directly.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Command-line","page":"Home","text":"bin/chopper.jl takes the options of chopper 0.14.1 (--help lists them), plus -o/--output (a file, gzip-compressed if it ends in .gz) and --summary (counts per filter on stderr). Start Julia with the threads to use:\n\njulia -t 4 --project=path/to/Chopper.jl path/to/Chopper.jl/bin/chopper.jl -q 10 -l 500 -i reads.fastq.gz > filtered.fastq\njulia -t 4 --project=... bin/chopper.jl -q 20 -i reads.fastq.gz -o filtered.fastq.gz --summary\ngunzip -c reads.fastq.gz | julia --project=... bin/chopper.jl --trim-approach trim-by-quality --cutoff 15\njulia --project=... bin/chopper.jl -c lambda.fasta --trim-approach split-by-low-quality --cutoff 15 --split-window 5 -l 50 -i reads.fastq\n\nMessages, warnings, errors and exit codes are those of chopper (\"Kept N reads out of M reads\" on stderr). Julia's start-up takes a few seconds; for many files, call chopper from a Julia session or script instead.","title":"Command line"},{"category":"section","location":"#Julia-API","page":"Home","text":"using Chopper\n\nstats = chopper(\"reads.fastq.gz\", \"filtered.fastq.gz\"; min_quality = 10, min_length = 500)\nstats.segments, stats.reads                  # chopper's \"Kept N reads out of M reads\"\nstats                                        # counts per filter (ChopperStats)\n\nchopper(\"reads.fastq\", \"trimmed.fastq\"; trim = SplitByLowQuality(15; window = 5), min_length = 50)\nchopper(\"reads.fastq\", \"clean.fastq\"; contaminants = \"lambda.fasta\", trim = FixedCrop(20, 20),\n        update_mods = true)\n\n# The same decisions for one read (raw Phred+33 quality line):\nf = ReadFilter(min_quality = 12, max_length = 30_000)\nread_segments(seq, qual; filter = f, trim = TrimByQuality(10))   # -> Vector{UnitRange{Int}}\nread_status(f, seq, qual)                                       # PASSED or the first failed filter\nmean_quality(codeunits(qual)), gc_content(codeunits(seq))\ntrim_ranges(BestReadSegment(15), qual)\n\nidx = ContaminantIndex(\"lambda.fasta\")       # reuse it across files\nis_contaminant(idx, seq), best_match_length(idx, seq)\nrewrite_mod_tags([\"MM:Z:C+m?,1,1;\", \"ML:B:C,200,10\"], seq, 11:19)\n\nchopper option Julia\n-q, --maxqual min_quality, max_quality\n-l, --maxlength min_length, max_length\n--mingc, --maxgc min_gc, max_gc\n-c contaminants (path or ContaminantIndex), contaminant_fraction (0.9)\n--trim-approach fixed-crop --headcrop H --tailcrop T trim = FixedCrop(H, T)\n--trim-approach trim-by-quality --cutoff C trim = TrimByQuality(C)\n--trim-approach best-read-segment --cutoff C trim = BestReadSegment(C)\n--trim-approach split-by-low-quality --cutoff C --split-window W trim = SplitByLowQuality(C; window = W)\n--update-mods, --inverse update_mods, inverse\n-t threads (at most Threads.nthreads())\n\nA trimming strategy is a subtype of TrimStrategy implementing trim_ranges!(out, strategy, qual), so new strategies plug into chopper without other changes (the tests define one).\n\nSemantics, as in chopper: the filters judge the whole, untrimmed read, in the order length, quality, GC, contaminants; trimming is applied afterwards to the reads they select, and only the minimum length is checked again on each segment (the mean quality of a segment is not recomputed). With inverse, the reads failing a filter are written (and trimmed) instead.","title":"Julia API"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citation-and-license","page":"Home","text":"Please cite chopper: De Coster W., Rademakers R. (2023) NanoPack2: population scale evaluation of long-read sequencing data. Bioinformatics 39(5): btad311. Chopper.jl is released under the MIT license (LICENSE). chopper is also MIT-licensed; its copyright notice and minimap2's are in NOTICE. The upstream version is recorded in UPSTREAM.toml.","title":"Citation and license"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Differences-and-validation","page":"Differences and validation","text":"","title":"Differences and validation"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Differences-from-chopper","page":"Differences and validation","text":"Details in docs/src/DIFFERENCES.md. In short:\n\ndeterministic output: records are written in input order with any number of threads; chopper writes them in the order its threads finish whenever -t > 1;\nlibrary API: strategies as types (invalid combinations of options cannot be expressed), 1-based UnitRange segments, statistics per filter returned as ChopperStats, errors as exceptions (ModTagError) instead of process::exit, no global state;\nrobustness: a read with an empty sequence is discarded by the length filter (chopper panics and stops writing, exit status 101); input compression recognised from the content also on stdin, including zstd; output to a file, optionally gzip-compressed;\n--inverse stops at the first failed filter as normal mode does (the read is kept either way); chopper evaluates every filter, including the alignment against contaminants;\ncontaminants without the minimap2 binary: Minimap2.jl maps the reads with the lr:hq preset as chopper's minimap2 crate does (docs/src/CONTAMINANTS.md).\n\nThe mean quality uses Julia's exp10 and log10, which can differ from the C library used by chopper in the last bit: only a read whose mean quality is exactly on a threshold could be classified differently (none in the validation data).","title":"Differences from chopper"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Validation","page":"Differences and validation","text":"test/runtests.jl (julia -t 4 --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()', 762 tests, about 4 minutes): chopper's own Rust unit tests, ported; byte-for-byte comparison with the output, stderr and exit status of the chopper 0.14.1 Linux binary on 35 cases (test/reference/, made by tools/make_reference.sh: every filter and trimming strategy, inverse, contaminants, warnings and errors, header edge cases, MM/ML/MN rewriting, broken tags), each with 1 and 4 threads; match lengths of the contaminant screen identical to minimap2 2.30's on chopper's test reads; compressed inputs (gzip, multi-member gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd, stdin).\nReal data (validation/), 200,000 ONT reads of the user's metabarcoding run (R10.4.1, dorado hac 5.2.0, 1.09 GB, tags in the header), against chopper 0.14.1 with one thread: output identical for -q 20; split-by-low-quality --cutoff 15 -l 200 (799,589 records); -q 15 best-read-segment --cutoff 18 -l 500; --mingc 0.35 --maxgc 0.45 --inverse -q 12 --maxlength 3000; with 1 and 4 threads.\nContamination screen, against chopper and minimap2 2.30 (the version in chopper 0.14.1): identical classification and output on chopper's test data and on 20,000 of those reads screened against amplicon sequences of the same sample (16,070 contaminants) and of another sample (none); see docs/src/CONTAMINANTS.md.\n\nSpeed on the 200,000 reads (Windows 11, 4 cores; chopper in WSL on the same machine):\n\n chopper 0.14.1 Chopper.jl\n-q 20, gzip input, 1 / 4 threads 4.5 / 5.3 s 5.4 / 4.3 s\n-q 20, plain input, 4 threads 1.6 s 0.84 s\n-c, 20,000 reads, 32 amplicons (16,070 contaminants) 46 s (1 thread) 24 s (4 threads)\n-c, 20,000 reads, 3 amplicons of another species 63 s (1 thread) 24 s (4 threads)\n\nWith gzip input both are limited by decompression (zlib-rs in chopper, zlib-ng here, in a separate task). The contamination screen is limited by Minimap2.jl, about 1.6 times slower than minimap2 per thread; reads are mapped in parallel.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"CONTAMINANTS/#Contamination-screen","page":"Contamination screen","text":"chopper's -c contaminants.fasta removes reads that align to a contaminant (typically the lambda phage DNA control strand of ONT kits) with at least 90% of the read length as matching bases. chopper maps every read with minimap2 (Rust binding minimap2 0.1.31, minimap2 2.30) configured as Aligner::builder().preset(LrHq).with_cigar().with_index(fasta), and checks the \"match length\" (PAF column 10) of every reported alignment.\n\nChopper.jl does the mapping with Minimap2.jl, a native Julia port of minimap2 2.30, configured in the same way (src/contaminants.jl):\n\nIndex (ContaminantIndex): MinimapIndex of the contaminant sequences with the lr:hq minimizers (k = 19, w = 19); the mapping options are then updated from the index, as with_index does (occurrence threshold from the 2·10⁻⁴ quantile, clamped to 50–500).\nMapping: map_query with base-level alignment (F_CIGAR), which runs minimap2's seeding, chaining, chain selection and ksw2 alignment.\nDecision (Chopper): a read is a contaminant if the largest match length over its alignments is at least contaminant_fraction (0.9) times its length.\n\nThe index is only read while mapping, so the batches of chopper() share it across threads.\n\nUntil 2026-10-21 Chopper.jl had its own reimplementation of the same mapping (minimizers, chaining, banded alignment by anti-diagonals). Its match lengths differed from minimap2's for 0.4–61% of the aligned reads (co-optimal paths, narrower bands, Z-drop points), although the classification agreed on the validation data. It was replaced by Minimap2.jl so that there is one mapper in the suite.","title":"Contamination screen"},{"category":"section","location":"CONTAMINANTS/#Agreement-with-minimap2-and-chopper","page":"Contamination screen","text":"test/test_contaminants.jl compares the match lengths with minimap2 2.30 (-x lr:hq -c, largest per read; tools/make_contaminant_reference.jl) on chopper's test.fastq (250 reads up to 77 kb, ~10% errors) against random_contam.fa (one 75 kb read): identical for all 250 reads (182 aligned).\n\nvalidation/compare_contaminants.jl, 20,000 ONT reads (ND2–COI amplicons, R10.4.1), minimap2 2.30 release binary (-x lr:hq -c --no-hash-name) and chopper 0.14.1 (-t 1 -c):\n\nContaminants Reads aligned (minimap2 / Julia) Match length identical Contaminants (chopper / Julia) Output identical\n3 amplicon sequences of another sample 15,761 / 15,761 20,000 / 20,000 0 / 0 yes\n32 amplicon sequences of the same and the other sample 19,060 / 19,060 20,000 / 20,000 16,070 / 16,070 yes\n\n--no-hash-name matters: minimap2 breaks ties between equal chains with a hash seeded by the read name, and chopper, like Chopper.jl, maps reads without their names. Without the option one read (95b66141…, 2,833 bases, fraction 0.88 against two nearly tied references, below the threshold either way) has a different match length; it was first reported here as a Minimap2.jl discrepancy (2026-10-22: it is not one).\n\nSpeed (20,000 reads, chopper() with 4 threads): 24 s with the 32 amplicons and 24 s with the 3 of another species; chopper took 46 s and 63 s with one thread.","title":"Agreement with minimap2 and chopper"}]
}
